# Special relativity 22 — The twin paradox with accelerating legs
#
# A traveller leaves Earth with a constant proper acceleration g = 9.81 m/s²
# for t₁ = 1.50 yr (Earth time), coasts for t_c = 2.00 yr, and decelerates for
# t₁ to rest at the far point; then she comes back the same way. In the Earth
# frame the proper velocity u = γv changes at the rate ±g (d(γv)/dt = g), so
# u(t) is piecewise linear in t, and
#
#     v = u / √(1 + u²/c²),   dτ/dt = 1/γ = 1 / √(1 + u²/c²).
#
# (a) Integrate dτ/dt over the whole trip to get the traveller's age, and
#     compare with the exact 4 (c/g) asinh(g t₁/c) + 2 t_c / γ_max.
# (b) How far away is the turning point? Integrate v, and compare with
#     2 (c²/g)(√(1 + (g t₁/c)²) − 1) + v_max t_c.
# (c) Solve the same trip as ODEs, u' = a(t), x' = v, τ' = 1/γ, with the
#     acceleration a(t) switching sign on the legs, and compare.
# (d) Without coasting (four accelerating legs), how long must each leg last
#     (Earth time) for the traveller to age exactly 40.0 yr, and how far does she
#     get? (Rindler §3.8; Taylor & Wheeler ch. 4, "the twin paradox".)

g = 9.81 m/s²
t1 = 1.50 yr
tc = 2.00 yr
T_leg = 2 t1 + tc                   # the Earth time of one way
T = 2 T_leg

# the proper velocity: +g for t1, coast, −g for 2 t1 (decelerate, turn back), coast, +g for t1
# (a piecewise formula over several lines needs the brackets: FRICTION S8)
u(t) = (if t < t1 then g t
       else if t < t1 + tc then g t1
       else if t < 3 t1 + tc then g (2 t1 + tc - t)
       else if t < 3 t1 + 2 tc then -g t1
       else g (t - T))
γ(t) = √(1 + u(t)² / c²)
v(t) = u(t) / γ(t)

# (a) the traveller's age: integrate over each leg (u has corners at the switches)
τ_trip = ∫ 1/γ(t) dt from 0 yr to T
γ_max = √(1 + (g t1 / c)²)
τ_exact = 4 c / g * asinh(g t1 / c) + 2 tc / γ_max
print "(a) Earth twin ages", T in yr to 6 digits
print "    traveller ages", τ_trip in yr to 10 digits, "  exact", τ_exact in yr to 10 digits
print "    top speed =", v(t1) in c to 10 digits

# (b) the turning point
x_far = ∫ v(t) dt from 0 yr to T_leg
print "(b) turning point at", x_far in ly to 10 digits,
      "  exact", 2 c² / g * (γ_max - 1) + v(t1) tc in ly to 10 digits
print "    back home: x(T) =", ∫ v(t) dt from 0 yr to T in ly to 3 digits

# (c) the same trip as ODEs with a switching acceleration
a(t) = (if t < t1 then g
       else if t < t1 + tc then 0 m/s²
       else if t < 3 t1 + tc then -g
       else if t < 3 t1 + 2 tc then 0 m/s²
       else g)
solve w' = a(t),
      x' = w / √(1 + w² / c²),
      τ' = 1 / √(1 + w² / c²)
  with w(0) = 0 m/s, x(0) = 0 ly, τ(0) = 0 yr
  for t from 0 yr to T
print "(c) ODE: traveller ages", τ(T) in yr to 10 digits, "  turning point", x(T_leg) in ly to 10 digits

# (d) four accelerating legs and a 40-year trip
τ_goal = 40.0 yr
solve 4 c / g * asinh(g t_a / c) = τ_goal for t_a from 0 yr to 1e6 yr
print "(d) each leg lasts", t_a in yr to 8 digits, "of Earth time; Earth ages", 4 t_a in yr to 8 digits
print "    farthest point", 2 c² / g * (√(1 + (g t_a / c)²) - 1) in ly to 8 digits
